<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Multi-targeted to match MCPBridge.Core itself (PRD §11). Reviewing this PR surfaced that the
         net8.0-windows leg ; the entire reason the multi-target exists ; had zero automated coverage:
         these tests only ever ran against the net10.0-windows build of Core. That is especially wrong
         for WireEnumNameConverter, which exists SPECIFICALLY because net8 lacks .NET 9+'s
         JsonStringEnumMemberNameAttribute, so the leg it was written for was the one leg never
         exercised. `dotnet test` now runs the whole suite once per TFM, which is what makes
         "byte-identical wire output on both legs" a claim CI can hold rather than one a live session
         confirmed once by hand. -->
    <TargetFrameworks>net10.0-windows;net8.0-windows</TargetFrameworks>
    <!--
      Multi-targeting these tests is the point: the net8.0-windows leg is the reason this project
      multi-targets at all, and WireEnumNameConverter exists precisely because net8's BCL lacks
      .NET 9+'s JsonStringEnumMemberNameAttribute.

      There is deliberately NO RollForward property here. An earlier version set
      RollForward=LatestMajor so the net8 assemblies could execute on the VM's only runtime (.NET
      10) ; that made `dotnet test` pass on both legs while running .NET 10's System.Text.Json
      TWICE, which is the one component whose net8/net10 difference this whole target exists for.
      It validated the net8 COMPILATION and nothing about net8 runtime behaviour.

      The VM now has the .NET 8 runtime (Microsoft.NETCore.App + Microsoft.WindowsDesktop.App
      8.0.30) alongside .NET 10, so the default roll-forward policy resolves the net8 leg to a real
      8.x runtime and the two legs genuinely differ. RuntimeTargetingTests asserts exactly that, per
      TFM, so this cannot silently regress if anyone reintroduces RollForward.

      Still exactly ONE SDK (see the skill's rule) ; a RUNTIME is not an SDK. Note the runtime
      installs to C:\Program Files\dotnet by default while the SDK lives in C:\dotnet10, and a
      .NET host only resolves frameworks from ITS OWN root (multi-level lookup was removed in
      .NET 7+), so the 8.0.30 framework folders were copied under C:\dotnet10\shared to be visible
      to the SDK's host. `dotnet ;;list-runtimes` (two hyphens ; MSB4025 forbids them in an XML
      comment) should show both 8.0.30 and 10.0.11.
    -->
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <RootNamespace>MCPBridge.Core.Tests</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.11.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.8.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\MCPBridge.Core\MCPBridge.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\MCPBridge.RevitAdapter\MCPBridge.RevitAdapter.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
